[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Is there a real auth backend behind API_ENDPOINT that will return the userId, or does login() stay a stub? → [39m
     [38;5;246mStub, async-shaped (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Recorded: async-shaped stub. [38;5;153mlogin()[39m returns a Promise resolving a fake [38;5;153muserId[39m, and the signature survives the real
  backend landing.

  Second question: how long the identity should outlive the login. "Persist" rules out memory-only, but there's a real
  gap between surviving a page refresh and surviving a closed browser — and since this store will likely hold a session
  token once the backend is real, the choice has a security consequence beyond convenience.

  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — survives refresh and in-app navigation, cleared when the tab closes. Buys you "the app remembers me
    while I'm using it" with no stale identity left on a shared machine. Costs: closing the tab logs you out, and a
    second tab starts logged out. Rules out nothing later.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — survives closing the browser entirely; shared across tabs. Buys you "stay logged in." Costs: the
    identity (and eventually the token) sits on disk until something explicitly clears it, readable by any script on the
    origin. This is the one with a real security consequence.
  - [1mIn-memory module only[0m — a plain module variable, no storage API. Simplest and leaks nothing, but a refresh wipes it,
    which contradicts "persist."

  [1mMy recommendation: [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m.[0m It satisfies persistence as you described it while keeping the blast radius of an
  XSS bug or a shared machine to one tab session. Swapping it for [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m later is a one-line change behind the
  store's interface, so this is cheap to reverse — which is exactly why I'd start with the tighter option.
